  - haskell-library — Bootstrap a single-package Haskell library under GHC 9.12.4 / GHC2024, with lens + generic-lens, a BSD-3 license, the project author's standard warning set, and an optional tasty test-suite. Depends on nix-haskell-flake for the dev shell. (installed)
  - claude-gitignore — Ensure .claude/ and CLAUDE.local.md are in .gitignore. Shared base module for Claude-related modules. (installed)
  - agent-gitignore — Ensure .claude/, .codex/, .agents/, and CLAUDE.local.md are in .gitignore. Shared base module for skill modules that publish into all three discovery directories. (installed)
  - haskell-keiro-project — Six-package Keiro seed with project-named domain paths, injected servant-health probes, OpenAPI generation, Hurl/Hinagata test setup, and optional initial Mori inventory on nix-haskell-flake. The blueprint implements the domain. (installed)
  - nix-haskell-flake — Optional explicit retained-runtime configuration shares one checked scope between production packages and the primary shell. Modular flake-parts Nix flake for Haskell projects, consuming the haskell-nix-dev base flake (prebuilt GHC/HLS/cabal toolchains). Every module-owned input is rev-pinned or follows that base, so each module version locks to byte-identical pins across projects and `nix flake update` cannot drift them. Project wiring lives in imported nix/*.nix modules, dependent Seihou modules can add managed package wiring through nix.package-module, and user customizations go in an unmanaged flake.module.nix, so template upgrades migrate without conflict. Toggleable process-compose, PostgreSQL, Redis, ClickHouse, treefmt-nix, and pre-commit-hooks with a commit-message newline-escape guard; the always-locked shared haskell-nix patch registry is available to custom wiring. The generated flake.module.nix.example includes a ready-to-uncomment, Linux-guarded dockerTools.buildLayeredImage block for building an OCI image of the project's executable. Optional nix.redpanda adds macOS-only redpanda-local-* scripts for a private, non-colliding Redpanda cluster on Apple Container (reusing the always-locked redpanda-container input), for tests that need a broker of their own instead of the shared machine-wide one. (installed)
  - haskell-cli-app — Bootstrap a tested two-package Haskell CLI app (reusable core library + CLI executable) under GHC2024, with GHC 9.12.4 as the HLS-backed default and GHC 9.14.1 as a secondary build shell. Generates valid package-local distribution metadata and workspace-aware Nix package/check outputs on the latest nix-haskell-flake. (installed)
  - repo-dir — Create a new directory `<repo.parentDir>/<repo.name>` and bootstrap it as a git repository with a matching GitHub repo by running `seihou run git-init` inside it (so git-init's manifest, .gitignore, and initial commit live in the new repo, not the directory you ran from). The GitHub owner is resolved from `git.githubOwner`, so per-context config (`seihou config set git.githubOwner <org-or-user> --context <ctx>`) picks the user or organization for the active context. (installed)
  - exec-plan — Claude skill for creating, implementing, and managing execution plans (ExecPlans) — self-contained design documents that guide implementation of features and system changes. (installed)


## Blueprint Identity

Name: keiro-upgrade
Version: 0.11.0
Description: Upgrade guidance for projects consuming Keiro, the event-sourcing framework and durable workflow engine. One edge per released version window that needs judgement work, with upstream cohort edges entailed so a project that has never heard of Kiroku still crosses them exactly once.


## Migration Edge

Step 2 of 2
From library version: 0.21.0.0
To library version: 0.22.0.0




## Reference Files

The blueprint declares these shared library-upgrade references:

  - keiro-cohort-versions.md — Which upstream cohort each Keiro release pairs with, how to read what this project actually resolved, deprecated upstream releases, and which PostgreSQL majors each layer covers.

These files are readable at: /private/tmp/nix-shell.lhWPDx/keiro-blueprint-preflight-r90mtzbs/.seihou/modules/keiro-upgrade/files — open them directly with your file tools before asking the user.


## Shared Blueprint Guidance

You are upgrading a project that consumes **Keiro** — an event-sourcing
framework and durable workflow engine for Haskell — across one released version
edge.

## The package set moves together

Keiro publishes seven packages under **one shared version**: `keiro-core`,
`keiro`, `keiro-pgmq`, `keiro-migrations`, `keiro-test-support`, `keiro-dsl`,
and `keiro-ops`. They are released as a set and their internal bounds are locked
to each other, so a project cannot hold `keiro` at one release and
`keiro-migrations` at another. An edge labelled `0.12.0.0 -> 0.13.0.0` therefore
moves **every** Keiro package this project depends on.

Use `files/keiro-cohort-versions.md` to see which upstream cohort each Keiro
release pairs with.

## Keiro sits on top of other libraries

Keiro composes Kiroku (the event store), Keiki (the pure functional core), and
Shibuya (message transport). A breaking change in one of them reaches this
project through Keiro's version space, and most projects on Keiro have never
named those libraries directly.

You do not have to know that map. When a Keiro edge requires an upstream
library's edge, it declares that edge, and Seihou will have run it **before**
this one, under that library's own prompt and reference files. So:

- **Assume the entailed work is already done.** Do not re-apply an upstream
  edge's instructions from inside a Keiro edge, and do not "fix" a change you
  find already made — it was made deliberately, by the step before you.
- **State overlaps explicitly.** Where a Keiro edge's guidance depends on an
  upstream change having landed, it says so; if you find that it has not, stop
  and report rather than doing the upstream work yourself.

## What a Keiro project looks like

Keiro projects vary widely in which surfaces they use, and an edge often touches
only one:

- **Aggregates and commands** — `runCommand`, deciders, codecs, snapshots.
- **Process managers, routers, and timers** — subscription workers with ack
  policies.
- **Durable workflows** — journaled steps, awakeables, child workflows.
- **Read models and projections** — catalog-bound projections, rebuild groups,
  external read contracts.
- **Messaging** — inbox, outbox, Kafka or PGMQ adapters.
- **The typed DSL** — `.keiro` specifications compiled by `keiro-dsl` into a
  generated layer plus explicit holes.
- **Migrations** — `keiro-migrate`, or an application runner that composes
  Keiro's migration plan.

Find which of these the project actually uses before assuming an edge's
instruction applies to it. Reporting an edge not applicable because the project
has not adopted the surface it changes is a correct, useful outcome.

## Generated code is not yours to hand-edit

If this project uses `keiro-dsl`, part of its source is **generated** from
`.keiro` specifications, and the generated files say so in a header. Never edit
a generated file to satisfy an edge. Change the specification, or the hand-owned
holes, and regenerate with the project's own scaffold command. An edge that
requires regeneration says so and names the command; if regeneration produces a
diff you did not expect, report it rather than reverting it.

## Database work stops at the runbook

Keiro owns SQL migrations, applied by `keiro-migrate` or by an application
runner that composes Keiro's plan after Kiroku's. **You do not migrate a
database that holds real data.** Establish from read-only evidence whether this
project has one and whether an edge affects it, then report exactly what its
operator must run — and stop. A local, disposable database the project's own
tooling recreates from scratch is the exception.

## Ground rules

- **Read before you edit.** Keiro's surface is large and this project uses a
  small part of it. Find real call sites before assuming a symbol is in use.
- **A clean build is not a search.** Several changes in this cohort add
  constructors to exported sum types. A non-exhaustive `case` is a warning
  unless the project builds with `-Werror`, so grep for constructors rather than
  trusting the compiler to find every site.
- **Do not widen the change.** Fix what this edge names. Unrelated warnings,
  formatting, and other libraries' version bumps are out of scope.
- **Prove it with the project's own commands.** Use the build and test commands
  this repository actually defines. Report any you could not run, and why,
  rather than claiming a pass.



## Instructions for This Edge

# Keiro 0.21.0.0 → 0.22.0.0

## Precondition

The consumer resolves Keiro 0.22.0.0 and previously used 0.21.0.0. Keep all
eight published Keiro packages aligned. Inspect direct imports, generated
services, operational embedding, and owned database migration composition.
Report inapplicability for unused surfaces instead of inventing adaptations.
Use Mori to locate upstream sources and verify actual resolved versions.

## Upstream cohort and database cutover

This edge entails the namespace cutover from mori://shinzui/kiroku:
`kiroku-upgrade` 0.11.0.1 → 0.12.0.0. Follow that edge for migration 0016,
subscription constructors, low-level integrations, and stopped-worker cutover.
The framework's Kiroku-plus-Keiro plan contains 48 migrations; PGMQ is a
separately composed component and is not included in that count.

Resolve Store `^>=0.12.0.0`, migrations `^>=0.7.2.0`, adapter `^>=0.7.0.0`,
and metrics `^>=0.4.0.0` where used, from mori://shinzui/kiroku. Resolve Core
`^>=0.12.0.0` from mori://shinzui/shibuya/packages/shibuya-core. Complete
`Ingested` records supply `deliveryStatus = pure DeliveryCurrent`; construct
`AppConfig` by updating `defaultAppConfig` to preserve its metrics defaults.

Resolve adapter `^>=0.18.0.0` from
mori://shinzui/shibuya-pgmq-adapter/packages/shibuya-pgmq-adapter and the
PGMQ family `>=0.7.0.0 && <0.8` from mori://shinzui/pgmq-hs. Custom `Pgmq`
interpreters handle `PeekMessages`, `PeekArchivedMessages`, `LookupMessage`,
`LookupArchivedMessage`, and `QueueMetricsUnvalidated`. Exhaustive
`ReconcileAction` matches handle `DetectedQueueNameCollision` and
`UnsupportedNotifyOnPartitionedQueue`. Remove conflicting orphan JSON
instances for domain types now encoded upstream.

Apply the released PGMQ component's append-only migration 0007 through the
consumer's existing migration composition before queue traffic. It suppresses
partition insert notifications; retain durable polling. Ordinary queues retain
crash fail-open behavior. Adapter DLQ envelopes add `original_queue`; preserve
existing payload and structured reason handling. Keiro's existing DLQ commands
retain their behavior; adopting upstream managed-DLQ administration is optional.

Do not rewrite historical migrations or checksums. Classify persistent databases
read-only and report the required operator cutover; rehearse on a restored clone.
Only established disposable, unshared databases may be migrated automatically.

## Operational embedding and HTTP clients

- Complete `RunCommandOptions` records supply `inspectionProvenance`; prefer
  default updates, keeping `NoInspectionProvenance` unless deliberately opting
  into bounded legacy capture. Complete `ProcessManagerInspector` records supply
  `provenanceMode`; reaction descriptors use `NoReactionProvenance`, never
  invented legacy provenance.
- Complete `AppHooks` records supply `aggregates` and `processManagers`;
  `emptyAppHooks` leaves them disabled. Supply catalogs only for application-owned
  mounted types. `pm types` and `GET /pm/types` discover these mounts without reads.
- Handle `OpsOutcome.InspectionFailed` in exhaustive outcome matches. Workflow
  show/steps/journal use structured typed inspection failures.
- Complete `InspectionLimits` records supply `deliveryDeadlineSeconds` (default
  30). Keep computation, response size and delivery budgets enabled.
- Default workflow browsing uses opaque primary-key `wf-key2` cursors; discard
  saved old default cursors and restart at the first page. Legacy listing and
  creation-order `wf-created` semantics remain. Filtered/creation reads still
  need host deadlines; row limits do not bound SQL work.
- Workflow list/show JSON adds `status_class`; steps add `recorded_at`. Show
  orders children and awakeables by their owner-table creation keys. Shard
  observations add database-clock timestamps and lease age. Adapt strict decoders.
- `pgmq dlq read` reserves messages for 30 seconds: unforced CLI invocation
  previews and exits 1; deliberate reservation needs `--force`. HTTP uses POST
  and explicit JSON confirmation, with nonempty `application/json` bodies.
- Mutation routes reject foreign, malformed, null, or multiple origins before
  effects. Configure the public HTTPS origin explicitly behind TLS proxies.
  GET/HEAD reads, empty previews and no-Origin clients retain their contracts.
- WebSocket first-page feeds retain fresh schema checks, bounded resources and
  `view_retiring` during cleanup. Reconnect/recover through the owning read path;
  an incomplete response can terminate at its delivery deadline. Preserve child
  authorization when composing `/keiro`, `/kiroku`, `/shibuya`, and `/pgmq` mounts.

## DSL library clients and generated services

Handle `AggregateStructuralEnum` and `StructuralEnumEqualityGuards` in exhaustive
matches. Complete `StructuralProjection` records supply `rootEnum = Nothing`
for prior projections; complete `ScaffoldRecord` and `WorkspaceRecord` records
supply `structuralEnumEqualities = []` for prior records. `AggregateMapped`
remains a bundled bidirectional pattern covering both mapped representations.
The new witness-name helpers are additive exports.

Regenerate using the consumer's existing scaffold/workspace commands with
0.22.0.0. Preserve create-once holes and real history-backed witnesses. Candidate
structural enum equality now emits exact finite wire-key root projections,
owner/key conformance laws, declaration-specific fold evidence, and
`structural-enum-equality` ledger rows. Review release/event-history/register/
fold/snapshot qualification before adopting equality or removing a downstream
native-enum adapter. Data-only mapped and unrelated fold identities retain bytes.
Structural and nominal literal-guarded harnesses select fixtures by canonical
key; fixture declaration order is not the contract. Do not fabricate qualifying
histories or weaken fixture/law obligations. Language 5 stays stable and Language
6 stays candidate; preserve the selected language and feature policy.

The 0.21.0.0 erratum is documentation of that prior release. Consumers that
already adapted it need not repeat it; lagging clients should follow the
existing 0.20.0.0 → 0.21.0.0 edge, including all BehaviorContract regeneration.

## Validation

Use the consumer's development environment for builds, exhaustive-pattern checks,
generated conformance/replay tests, migration dry-run/verification, queue delivery
and DLQ checks, and any used CLI/HTTP/WebSocket surfaces. In this Keiro repository
the proving commands are:

```sh
nix develop -c z3 --version
nix develop -c cabal build all --enable-tests --enable-benchmarks
nix develop -c cabal test keiro-pgmq-test keiro-migrations-test keiro-ops-test keiro-ops-http-test --test-show-details=direct
nix develop -c cabal test keiro-dsl:tests --test-show-details=direct
nix develop -c just release-gate
nix develop -c nix flake check
```

Report exact resolved versions, changed surfaces, checks and pending operator
work. Do not drop persistent databases, edit migration history, add `allow-newer`
to conceal an incompatible cohort, overwrite application holes, enable schema
drift to hide a mismatch, or promote candidate language features automatically.



## Workflow

1. Inspect how the project actually uses the library at the source version.
   Do not assume every API named in the guidance is present.
2. Read relevant mounted reference files when available. If they are not
   mounted, ask the user for anything essential and never claim to have read it.
3. Make only the changes needed for 0.21.0.0 -> 0.22.0.0.
   Preserve unrelated user changes and do not pre-apply later migration edges.
   Project-owned seed source/configuration may be edited when this edge requires
   it; do not rerun templates or alter seed receipts to erase authored changes.
4. Update source, configuration, and tests that are directly affected by this
   edge. Avoid broad cleanup unrelated to the upgrade.
5. Run the most relevant project validation available for this library change.
   Report checks you could not run and why.
6. Before exiting, summarize changed files, validation results, and any work
   that remains for the user or later migration steps.


## If This Edge Does Not Apply

An edge states its own precondition. If this project does not meet it — the
library is not used here, the feature this edge upgrades was never adopted, the
change is already present — the correct action is to change nothing and report
that. This is the ordinary case for an edge the project reached indirectly:
a project that depends on one library only through another may never use the
upgraded library's API itself.

Do not make speculative edits to justify the step, and do not exit with an
error: an error means the provider failed, halts the remaining edges, and asks
the user to retry.

To report it, write one line explaining why to:

/private/tmp/nix-shell.lhWPDx/keiro-blueprint-preflight-r90mtzbs/.seihou/.migrate-signal

If you cannot write files, end your reply with a line of exactly this form:

    SEIHOU: not-applicable <one-line reason>

Seihou records the attempt with that outcome, prints your reason, and continues
to the next edge. The edge is not marked done, so it runs again once the
precondition is met.


